{"files": {"SKILL.md": "## Coding Execution Playbook\n\n### 1) Pre-change checks\n- Load the available docs, task context, and existing records before coding.\n- Mirror the request exactly; do not reinterpret requirements or add extras.\n- When the workspace is remote, read and write via `ssh` / `scp`; assume local tools do not see that tree.\n- Begin by listing the workspace once with ONE terminal call combining commands; trust the initial layout and do not re-search later.\n- Read the task-named files in parallel, and stop reading once you can act.\n\n### 2) Change strategy\n- Keep the patch minimal and localized to the requested target.\n- Reuse patterns from nearby code that already works before inventing a new approach.\n- Protect existing correct behavior while applying the fix.\n\n### 3) Tooling style\n- Before each wave of calls, identify independent work and send those tool calls together in one message.\n- Serialize only when a later step depends on an earlier result.\n\n### 4) Validation and handoff\n- Run relevant checks and treat results as the source of truth.\n- Re-verify the modified target after each meaningful change.\n- Be careful with boundary checks.\n- Commit what you've changed.\n- Communicate briefly and clearly.\n- Leave concise, verified notes in the workspace notes area for stable observations and recurring patterns.\n\n### 5) Learning principles\n- **Evidence over intuition:** prefer observed outcomes over confidence.\n- **Short feedback loops:** many small cycles beat one large speculative pass.\n- **Explicit memory:** write down lessons so they transfer to future runs.\n- **Error as signal:** failures are training data, not dead ends.\n- **Generalization over overfitting:** optimize for recurring patterns, not one-off wins.\n\n### 6) Git discipline\n- Branch before editing using the branch name the ticket specifies; if another task branch already exists, branch from that newest branch, not from default.\n- Set `git config user.name` and `user.email` before the first commit (missing identity can block commits quietly).\n- Stage explicit paths only (`git add path/to/file`); avoid bulk `git add -A`.\n- Use one-line commit messages; do not amend, rebase, or squash unless the ticket or policy explicitly allows it.\n- Add a sensible `.gitignore` early once when needed (e.g. `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`, `.pytest_cache/`).\n\n### 7) Persistent notes\n- Use the workspace notes area for stable observations (broken assumptions, tooling quirks, recurring patterns).\n- Write notes when surprises happen, keep one line per observation, and update in place instead of duplicating.\n- Record only verified facts; when live data contradicts a note, replace the stale line.\n\n### 8) Exit contract\n- **Checks green + commit on disk = stop.** Do not keep iterating for polish or incidental cleanup once the ask is satisfied and the change is committed.\n- A narrative **summary alone is not completion** unless the ticket is documentation-only; completion means what the environment can verify (tests/checks, landed commit, named deliverables).\n"}, "schema_version": 1}